const faqs = [
  { q: "Who can get a caste certificate in Uttar Pradesh?", a: "Any UP resident who belongs to a caste notified as Scheduled Caste (SC), Scheduled Tribe (ST) or Other Backward Class (OBC) in the Uttar Pradesh state list can apply. The caste must appear in the UP list specifically — a caste recognised in another state may not be listed in UP, and the certificate is issued on the basis of the state list." },
  { q: "How much does a caste certificate cost in UP?", a: "Applying yourself on eSathi costs ₹10–15. Through a CSC you pay ₹30 plus a facilitation charge of about ₹30–50. Nothing beyond this is official, so refuse any higher demand from agents." },
  { q: "How long is a UP caste certificate valid?", a: "SC and ST certificates are valid for a lifetime — caste does not change. OBC Non-Creamy Layer certificates are different: creamy layer status depends on current family income, so an OBC NCL certificate is typically valid for 1–3 years and institutions often ask for one issued in the current financial year." },
  { q: "What documents prove my caste?", a: "The strongest proof is an existing caste certificate of your father or another close paternal relative. If the family has none, a certificate from the Gram Pradhan or local councillor confirming your caste, along with school records or older revenue documents mentioning caste, is used and then verified by the Lekhpal in the field." },
  { q: "How many days does a caste certificate take?", a: "Normally 7–15 working days. The Lekhpal verifies your caste locally — speaking to neighbours and checking records — before the Tehsildar signs. Expect delays during the July–September scholarship season." },
  { q: "What is the difference between OBC and OBC Non-Creamy Layer?", a: "OBC is the caste category; Non-Creamy Layer (NCL) is an income-based sub-status within it. Reservation benefits in jobs and admissions are available only to the non-creamy layer, broadly families below the prescribed income ceiling (₹8 lakh per year for central purposes). The certificate records both your caste and, for OBC applicants, your NCL status." },
  { q: "My caste certificate application was rejected. What now?", a: "Open eSathi and read the rejection remark. Common causes are weak caste proof, a missing or unsigned self-declaration, or a name mismatch with Aadhaar. Correct the specific issue and apply again — there is no penalty for reapplying." },
  { q: "Can I apply for a caste certificate from another district?", a: "Apply in the district and tehsil where you permanently reside and where your family's caste records and local verification are possible. The Lekhpal of your own area verifies caste, so applying from an unrelated tehsil usually leads to the file being returned." },
];
---
<Base lang="en" title="Caste Certificate UP (Jati Praman Patra): SC/ST & OBC Apply Online, Documents, Validity" description="How to apply for a caste certificate (jati praman patra) online in Uttar Pradesh: SC/ST and OBC Non-Creamy Layer documents, fees ₹10–15, validity, processing time, status check and download on eSathi." path="/en/caste-certificate/" altPath="/caste-certificate/" faqs={faqs} updated="22 October 2026">
<div class="content-col">
  <h1 class="page-title">Caste Certificate in UP — Apply Online on eSathi</h1>

  <section class="box">
    <h2>What is a caste certificate?</h2>
    <p>A caste certificate (जाति प्रमाण पत्र, jati praman patra) is the official document that proves a person belongs to a particular caste recognised by the Government of Uttar Pradesh as Scheduled Caste (SC), Scheduled Tribe (ST) or Other Backward Class (OBC). It is issued by the Tehsildar of your tehsil after a local verification, and it carries the officer's digital signature.</p>
    <p>The certificate is asked for wherever caste-based entitlement is claimed:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Reservation in jobs and admissions</strong> — SC/ST/OBC quota seats in government recruitment, universities and professional courses require this proof.</li>
      <li><strong>Scholarships</strong> — UP post-matric and other caste-linked scholarships are processed against the caste certificate.</li>
      <li><strong>Fee concessions and age relaxation</strong> — many examinations and schemes give relaxations only against a valid certificate.</li>
      <li><strong>Elections and welfare schemes</strong> — contesting reserved seats and several housing and welfare benefits also need it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>One point is essential before you apply: the caste must be on the <strong>Uttar Pradesh state list</strong>. Caste lists differ from state to state, so a caste notified as OBC in a neighbouring state may not be notified in UP. If your family migrated to UP, check the state list first — the certificate can only be issued for a listed caste.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="box">
    <h2>Caste certificate at a glance</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="info">
      <tr><th>Fee via eSathi (self-apply)</th><td>₹10–15</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Fee via CSC</th><td>₹30 + ₹30–50 facilitation charge</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Processing time</th><td>7–15 working days</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Validity — SC / ST</th><td>Lifetime</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Validity — OBC Non-Creamy Layer</th><td>1–3 years (income-linked, see below)</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Where to apply</th><td>esathi.up.gov.in (Citizen Login on edistrict.up.gov.in)</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Issued by</th><td>Tehsildar of your tehsil, after Lekhpal verification</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Helpline</th><td>0522-2304706 · ceghelpdesk@gmail.com</td></tr>
    </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="box">
    <h2>SC/ST and OBC Non-Creamy Layer — the difference that matters</h2>
    <p>Although both are called "caste certificates", the SC/ST certificate and the OBC certificate work differently, and the documents and validity follow from that difference.</p>
    <h3>SC and ST certificates</h3>
    <p>Caste by birth does not change, and neither does SC/ST status. Once issued, the certificate remains valid <strong>for life</strong> — you apply once and use it permanently. Verification concentrates on one question: does this family actually belong to the claimed caste? The Lekhpal confirms it through local enquiry and records.</p>
    <h3>OBC Non-Creamy Layer certificates</h3>
    <p>For OBC applicants, reservation benefits are limited to the <strong>Non-Creamy Layer (NCL)</strong> — families below a prescribed income and status ceiling (broadly ₹8 lakh annual income for central purposes). Because a family's income can rise over time, NCL status is treated as temporary: the certificate is generally valid for <strong>1–3 years</strong>, and universities, recruiting boards and scholarship departments frequently insist on a certificate from the current financial year. Along with caste proof, an OBC applicant's family income is also examined, which is why the income documents listed below become important for OBC applications.</p>
    <div class="warn"><strong>Practical advice:</strong> if you need an OBC NCL certificate for a specific admission or recruitment, apply for a fresh one in that financial year instead of relying on an older certificate — old NCL certificates are a very common reason applications stall at document verification.</div>
  </section>

  <section class="box">
    <h2>Documents required for a caste certificate</h2>
    <ul class="checklist">
      <li>Aadhaar card of the applicant</li>
      <li>Recent passport-size photograph</li>
      <li>Ration card</li>
      <li>Self-declaration form (swa-pramanit ghoshna patra), filled and signed — upload as JPEG</li>
      <li><strong>Caste proof:</strong> father's or a close paternal relative's existing caste certificate — the strongest and most commonly used proof</li>
      <li>If no family member has a certificate: certificate from the Gram Pradhan (village) or local councillor confirming your caste</li>
      <li>Supporting records where available: school leaving certificate or land/revenue records that mention caste</li>
      <li>For OBC NCL applications: income proof of the family (salary slip, income certificate or bank statement)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="note"><strong>File rules:</strong> each upload must be under 100KB, in JPEG or PDF. The self-declaration is the exception — it is accepted as a <strong>JPEG image only</strong>, not as a PDF. Prepare and sign it before you start; the <a href="/en/self-declaration-form/">self-declaration guide</a> explains the format.</div>
  </section>

  <section class="box">
    <h2>How to apply for a caste certificate online — step by step</h2>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><div class="step-num"></div><div class="step-body">
        <h3>Register or log in on eSathi</h3>
        <p>Open esathi.up.gov.in. New users click "New User Registration" (नवीन उपयोगकर्ता पंजीकरण), fill name (exactly as on Aadhaar), date of birth, address, district and an Aadhaar-linked mobile number, create a 6–8 character login ID, verify the Aadhaar OTP and set a password. Existing users log in directly with login ID, password and captcha.</p>
        <figure class="shot"><img src="/images/esathi-login.webp" alt="eSathi citizen login page of Uttar Pradesh" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The eSathi login page — registration and login both start here</figcaption></figure>
      </div></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="step-num"></div><div class="step-body">
        <h3>Open the certificate services list</h3>
        <p>From the dashboard, go to certificate services (प्रमाण पत्र सेवाएं), which lists all revenue department certificates available online.</p>
      </div></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="step-num"></div><div class="step-body">
        <h3>Select "Caste Certificate" and your category</h3>
        <p>Choose the caste certificate (जाति प्रमाण पत्र) service and select your category — SC, ST or OBC. For OBC, the form proceeds on the Non-Creamy Layer basis, so keep the family's income details ready.</p>
      </div></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="step-num"></div><div class="step-body">
        <h3>Fill the applicant's details</h3>
        <p>Enter name, father's name, date of birth, gender and complete address — district, tehsil, block, village or ward. Write your caste name exactly as it appears in the UP state list; free-text variations cause verification queries.</p>
      </div></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="step-num"></div><div class="step-body">
        <h3>Enter the caste proof details</h3>
        <p>If your father or a relative already holds a caste certificate, enter its certificate number and issuing details in the form. This links your application to an existing verified record and noticeably speeds up verification. If the family is applying for the first time, this section stays based on the Pradhan or councillor certificate.</p>
      </div></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="step-num"></div><div class="step-body">
        <h3>Select rural or urban</h3>
        <p>Choose ग्रामीण (rural) or नगरीय (urban) according to your actual residence. This routes the file to the correct verifying officer — the Lekhpal for villages, the Revenue Inspector for urban areas.</p>
      </div></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="step-num"></div><div class="step-body">
        <h3>Upload the documents</h3>
        <p>Upload the photograph, Aadhaar, ration card, caste proof and the signed self-declaration (JPEG only). Every file under 100KB and clearly readable — the Lekhpal and Tehsildar decide from these scans before the field visit.</p>
      </div></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="step-num"></div><div class="step-body">
        <h3>Review, submit and pay</h3>
        <p>Check the preview against your Aadhaar and caste proof, submit, and pay the ₹10–15 fee through PAYGOV (UPI, net banking or debit card). If a payment debits but shows pending, wait 24–48 hours for reconciliation instead of paying twice.</p>
      </div></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="step-num"></div><div class="step-body">
        <h3>Note your SRN</h3>
        <p>The portal generates a Service Request Number (SRN), also sent by SMS. Keep it safe — it is your tracking reference and the first thing the helpline will ask for.</p>
      </div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="note">What happens next: the Lekhpal verifies your caste in the field — he speaks to neighbours, checks the Pradhan's records and any old documents — and reports to the Tehsildar, who issues the certificate with a digital signature. For first-time family applications this enquiry is more detailed, so allow the full 15 working days.</div>
  </section>

  <section class="box">
    <h2>Fees: applying yourself vs through a CSC</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="data">
      <tr><th>Route</th><th>Government fee</th><th>Extra charges</th><th>Total you should pay</th></tr>
      <tr><td>eSathi (self-apply online)</td><td>₹10–15</td><td>None</td><td>₹10–15</td></tr>
      <tr><td>CSC / Jan Seva Kendra</td><td>₹30</td><td>₹30–50 facilitation</td><td>₹60–80</td></tr>
    </table>
    </div>
    <p>If you use a CSC, the operator applies through their own operator login — never hand over your personal eSathi password. Read more in the <a href="/en/csc-guide/">CSC guide</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="box">
    <h2>Check status and download the certificate</h2>
    <p>Track your application with the SRN — the complete method and the meaning of each status (Under Verification, Forwarded to Tehsildar, Approved, Certificate Generated) are covered in the <a href="/en/#status-check">status check section of the homepage guide</a>. When the status reaches <strong>Certificate Generated</strong>, log in to eSathi, open <strong>Completed Applications</strong>, click your application and use <strong>Print → Save as PDF</strong>. The signed PDF is the final certificate and can be printed any number of times; it also reaches DigiLocker's Issued Documents after a few days.</p>
    <figure class="shot"><img src="/images/status-page.webp" alt="Application status page on the eDistrict UP portal" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Checking application status with the SRN on the eDistrict UP portal</figcaption></figure>
  </section>

  <section class="box">
    <h2>Common rejection reasons — and how to fix them</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Weak caste proof</strong> — applying with no family certificate and no Pradhan/councillor certificate. Obtain one of the two before reapplying.</li>
      <li><strong>Caste not in the UP list</strong> — the claimed caste is notified in another state but not in UP. This cannot be fixed by resubmitting; check the state list first.</li>
      <li><strong>Self-declaration missing, unsigned or uploaded as PDF</strong> — sign it and upload a clear JPEG.</li>
      <li><strong>Name or father's name mismatch</strong> — details must match Aadhaar and the caste proof exactly, including spelling.</li>
      <li><strong>OBC income too high for NCL</strong> — if the family's income crosses the ceiling, the NCL certificate is refused; the remark states the income reason.</li>
      <li><strong>Wrong tehsil or rural/urban choice</strong> — the verifying officer is not the one for your area; correct the selection and resubmit.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Rejection carries no penalty and no extra fee. Read the remark in your eSathi account, fix that specific point and apply again. For a file pending beyond 15 working days, complain on the portal or call <strong>0522-2304706</strong> with your SRN.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="box">
    <h2>Related guides</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/en/income-certificate/">Income Certificate in UP</a> — needed alongside OBC NCL applications and for scholarships.</li>
      <li><a href="/en/domicile-certificate/">Domicile Certificate in UP</a> — residence proof, a common companion document in admissions and jobs.</li>
      <li><a href="/en/self-declaration-form/">Self-Declaration Form</a> — mandatory for caste applications; format and upload rules explained.</li>
      <li><a href="/en/csc-guide/">CSC / Jan Seva Kendra Guide</a> — applying through a CSC without overpaying.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</div>
